<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dijon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dijon is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the historic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. It was the seat of the Capetian dukes of Burgundy from the 11th century and of the Valois dukes from 1363, whose palace and tombs at the charterhouse of Champmol made it a centre of late Gothic art. It lies at the northern end of the Côte d'Or vineyards and is known for its mustard.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dijon.jpg" alt="Dijon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dijon_-_Puits_de_Mo%C3%AFse_-_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dijon lies in a plain drained by two small converging rivers, the Suzon, which crosses the town mostly underground from north to south, and the Ouche on its southern side. South of the town rises the côte, the hillside of vineyards that gives the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> its name. The city is about 310 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and has a climate that is oceanic with continental influence. The Canal de Bourgogne passes through the city and links the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonne-river">Yonne</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the north-west with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> to the south-east; these waterways were largely completed before the 19th century and were the main means of industrial transport until railways took over in the mid-19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest archaeological finds within the city limits date to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. Dijon later became a Roman settlement called Divio, which may mean sacred fountain, on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Saint Benignus, the city's apocryphal patron saint, is said to have introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to the area before being martyred.</p><p>The province was home to the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from the early 11th until the late 15th century, and Dijon was a place of great wealth and power and one of the major European centres of art, learning and science, with Dijon Abbey having great influence. The Palace of the Dukes of Burgundy now houses the city hall and the Museum of Fine Arts. In 1513 Swiss and Imperial armies invaded Burgundy and besieged Dijon, which the governor, Louis II de la Trémoille, defended. After long negotiations he persuaded the besiegers to withdraw. The population, which had called on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> for help, saw this as a miracle and began to venerate Notre-Dame de Bon-Espoir, Our Lady of Good Hope.</p><p>Dijon was occupied by anti-Napoleonic coalitions in 1814, by the Prussian army in 1870–71, and by Nazi Germany from June 1940. It was liberated by the French Army and the Resistance on 11 September 1944. Henry Darcy built a system of purified water for its citizens a quarter-century before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> had one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dijon is the prefecture of the Côte-d'Or department, with 161,830 inhabitants in the commune in 2023. Its historic centre keeps Capetian, Gothic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> buildings, half-timbered houses of the 12th to 15th centuries and roofs of glazed terracotta tiles in geometric patterns. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well-of-moses">Well of Moses</a>, a sculpture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claus-sluter">Claus Sluter</a>, is among its best-known works. In 2015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> registered the historic centre as part of the Climats, terroirs of Burgundy World Heritage site. Dijon mustard dates from 1856, when Jean Naigeon substituted verjuice for vinegar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dijon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Burgundy took shape in the late ninth and early tenth centuries in the western part of the old Burgundian lands, the region of Autun, <b>Dijon</b>, Beaune and Chalon-sur-Saône that remained within West Francia when Provence and Upper Burgundy formed separate kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rousseau, a watchmaker’s son from Geneva, won fame in 1750 with a prize essay for the Academy of <b>Dijon</b>, then quarrelled with most of the other philosophes and became the movement’s most searching critic of luxury and inequality.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Cappeau wrote the poem, &quot;Minuit, chrétiens&quot;, on 3 December 1847 during a stagecoach journey from Mâcon to <b>Dijon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-holy-night">O Holy Night</a></li></ul></section>
